# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in every working tree,
# including on Windows.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout translates text files on the way out, and
# any test that reads a shipped file as bytes sees content the same test sees
# as LF everywhere else. That is not hypothetical: the CRLF a Windows runner
# handed `test_fingerprint_reflects_the_raw_bytes_with_no_line_ending_
# normalization` got doubled into `\r\r\n`, which is not valid TOML, and the
# load failed on a malformed file instead of reporting the fingerprint the
# test exists to check. The rules data is parsed as TOML and fingerprinted
# with SHA-256 over its raw bytes, so which bytes land on disk is part of the
# contract rather than a display detail.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git detect genuinely binary files and leave them
# alone; `eol=lf` pins the working-tree ending for everything it does treat
# as text.
* text=auto eol=lf
